
// Ideas for zero-initialization and leaving things uninitialized

// Ideas:
x : int = void;
x : int = null;
x : int = default;
x : int = ?;

// For "zeroing" a variable, you would set it equal to `default` like:
x : int = default;
// The word "default" is used so that it can apply to symbols of many types, like structs etc.
// For instance:
bar :: struct {
    a : int = 6;
    s : string = "Hello world";
}
b : bar = default;
// In this case, after setting `b` to `default`, b.a would be equal to 6, and b.s would be "Hello world".
// Since in most cases, you don't want to have two default states for a struct (one with everything literally
// zeroed out, the other with the default values specified in the definition)


// And I am thinking if this `default` value should be assumed implicitly in declaration.
// For example, if you declare a variable like:
x : float;
// Then the compiler needs to decide if this should be zeroed or left uninitialized
// While forcing it to be zeroed would be safer, it also loses a lot of performance.
// So it might be better to instead make it uninitialized when declared without a value,
// and have the error checker ensure it is never used before being set.

// Also, I want to allow users to explicitly state that a variable is uninitialized.
// With the syntax above, it may not be clear there is a difference, like:
i : int = default; // This one is initialized to 0
j : int;           // This one is uninitialized

printl(i); // prints `0`
printl(j); // throws an error, `j` is uninitialized

// So while I don't think it should necessarily be required, there should be a value which
// represents uninitialized memory, that allows users to be explicit. Like:
i : int = default; // This one is initialized to 0
j : int = ?;       // This one is uninitialized

// Using `?` as the symbol may not be the best choice, maybe just changing it to `??` or `???`
// could be better. But the point is moreso just the fact that it explicitly shows in the code
// that the declaration does not initialize with any value
